questions.stem:
A method carries this comment: /** Returns the mean of two readings. The readings must be in the same unit. */ double mean(double a, double b) { return (a + b) / 2; }. What does a generated summary list show?
class Mean {
    /** Returns the mean of two readings. The readings must be in the same unit. */
    double mean(double a, double b) {
        return (a + b) / 2;
    }
}

A method is preceded by /* Computes the mean of two readings. */ instead of a documentation comment. What does a generated page record for that method?
class Mean {
    /* Computes the mean of two readings. */
    double mean(double a, double b) {
        return (a + b) / 2;
    }
}

A method is documented as /** @param width the box width in centimetres */ and then returns width times width. What is missing from the stated contract?
class Area {
    /**
     * @param width the box width in centimetres
     */
    int area(int width) {
        return width * width;
    }
}

A method takes one parameter called width, and its comment says @param length the box length. What does a generated page report?
class Area {
    /**
     * @param length the box length in centimetres
     */
    int area(int width) {
        return width * width;
    }
}

A method documents its guard with the comment /** Call only when {@code count < limit}. */ Why is the fragment wrapped in that inline tag?
class Guard {
    /**
     * Call only when {@code count < limit}.
     */
    void advance(int count, int limit) {
    }
}

A comment reads /** @return the trimmed form, as from {@link String#trim()} */. What does the generated page show in place of the link?
class Tidy {
    String s = " quiz ";

    /**
     * @return the trimmed form, as from {@link String#trim()}
     */
    String tidy() {
        return s.trim();
    }
}

A class documents a private helper with a full comment. Which statement about the default generated page is true?
class Cleaner {
    /**
     * Strips the padding from both ends.
     */
    private String scrub(String s) {
        return s.strip();
    }
}

A comment sits after the method's opening brace instead of before it. What does the page record for the method?
class Mean {
    double mean(double a, double b) {
        /** Returns the mean of two readings. */
        return (a + b) / 2;
    }
}

A method declares a result but ends with a bare return. What does the compiler say?
class Calc {
    int half(int v) {
        if (v % 2 == 0) {
            return v / 2;
        }
        return;
    }
}

class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println(new Calc().half(5));
    }
}

A method declares an int result but hands back text. What does the compiler say?
class Calc {
    int half(int v) {
        return "" + (v / 2);
    }
}

class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println(new Calc().half(5));
    }
}

A constructor is given a result type. What does the compiler say?
class Counter {
    int n;

    Counter int(int start) {
        n = start;
    }
}

class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println(new Counter(4).n);
    }
}

A method declares a broad fault it never raises. What does the program print?
class Reader {
    static String describe() throws java.io.IOException {
        return "ready";
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) throws Exception {
        System.out.println(Reader.describe());
    }
}

A method raises a checked fault but neither declares nor catches it. What happens?
class Loader {
    static String load() {
        throw new java.io.IOException("missing");
    }
}

class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println(load());
    }
}

A helper declares throws IOException and a caller must call it. Which two changes let that call stand without a try block?
class Loader {
    static String load() throws java.io.IOException {
        return "ready";
    }
}

A getter returns its internal array directly. What happens when a caller edits it?
class Report {
    private final int[] values = { 1, 2 };

    int[] values() {
        return values;
    }
}

class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Report r = new Report();
        int[] taken = r.values();
        taken[0] = 99;
        System.out.println(r.values()[0]);
    }
}

The getter builds a fresh copy of its array. What does the caller observe?
class Report {
    private final int[] values = { 1, 2 };

    int[] values() {
        return java.util.Arrays.copyOf(values, values.length);
    }
}

class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Report r = new Report();
        int[] taken = r.values();
        taken[0] = 99;
        System.out.println(r.values()[0] + " " + taken[0]);
    }
}

The constructor copies the array it is handed. What does the program print?
class Report {
    final int[] values;

    Report(int[] start) {
        values = java.util.Arrays.copyOf(start, start.length);
    }
}

class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int[] seed = { 1, 2 };
        Report r = new Report(seed);
        seed[0] = 99;
        System.out.println(r.values[0] + " " + seed[0]);
    }
}

An immutable list is built with one null element. What does the program print?
class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        try {
            java.util.List.of("a", null);
            System.out.println("made");
        } catch (NullPointerException e) {
            System.out.println("refused");
        }
    }
}

A write is attempted on a list built by the immutable factory. What does the program print?
class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        java.util.List<String> names = java.util.List.of("a", "b");
        try {
            names.add("c");
            System.out.println("added");
        } catch (UnsupportedOperationException e) {
            System.out.println("refused");
        }
    }
}

A list is wrapped around an array with the fixed size factory. What is printed?
class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        java.util.List<String> names = java.util.Arrays.asList("a", "b");
        try {
            names.add("c");
        } catch (UnsupportedOperationException e) {
            System.out.print("size refused, ");
        }
        System.out.println(names.size());
    }
}

The list is a view over the array, and a slot is replaced through it. What is printed?
class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        String[] seats = { "a", "b" };
        java.util.List<String> view = java.util.Arrays.asList(seats);
        view.set(1, "z");
        System.out.println(seats[1] + " " + view.get(1) + " " + view.size());
    }
}

A list is wrapped as read only, then the original list grows. What does the wrapper report?
class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        java.util.List<String> live = new java.util.ArrayList<>(java.util.List.of("a"));
        java.util.List<String> view = java.util.Collections.unmodifiableList(live);
        live.add("b");
        System.out.println(view.size() + " " + view.get(1));
    }
}

A growable copy is made from an immutable list, then extended. What is printed?
class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        java.util.List<String> fixed = java.util.List.of("a");
        java.util.List<String> copy = new java.util.ArrayList<>(fixed);
        copy.add("b");
        System.out.println(fixed.size() + " " + copy.size() + " " + copy);
    }
}

An in place sort is asked to reorder an immutable list. What does the program print?
class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        java.util.List<String> names = java.util.List.of("b", "a");
        try {
            java.util.Collections.sort(names);
            System.out.println(names);
        } catch (UnsupportedOperationException e) {
            System.out.println("refused, size " + names.size());
        }
    }
}

A wrapper that must hold a value is given a null. What does the program print?
class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        try {
            java.util.Optional.of(null);
            System.out.println("wrapped");
        } catch (NullPointerException e) {
            System.out.println("refused");
        }
    }
}

A wrapper is built from a value that may be missing. What does the program print?
class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        String missing = null;
        java.util.Optional<String> box = java.util.Optional.ofNullable(missing);
        System.out.println(box.isPresent() + " " + box.isEmpty());
    }
}

A wrapper may or may not hold a value, and a stand in is supplied. What is printed?
class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        java.util.Optional<String> found = java.util.Optional.ofNullable("here");
        java.util.Optional<String> gone = java.util.Optional.ofNullable(null);
        System.out.println(found.orElse("none") + " " + gone.orElse("none"));
    }
}

Both wrappers are mapped, and the mapping step prints as it runs. What is shown?
class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        java.util.function.Function<String, Integer> len = s -> {
            System.out.println("measuring " + s);
            return s.length();
        };
        java.util.Optional<String> found = java.util.Optional.of("quiz");
        java.util.Optional<String> gone = java.util.Optional.empty();
        System.out.print(found.map(len).orElse(-1) + " ");
        System.out.println(gone.map(len).orElse(-1));
    }
}

Three names are joined, one of which is null. What does the program print?
class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        String[] names = { "al", null, "cy" };
        System.out.println(String.join("-", names));
    }
}

A reference that may be null is turned into text. What does the program print?
class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        String name = null;
        System.out.println("[" + String.valueOf(name) + "]");
    }
}

A helper is asked for the text of a reference that may be missing. What is printed?
class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        String present = "here";
        String missing = null;
        System.out.println(java.util.Objects.toString(missing, "none") + " "
                + java.util.Objects.toString(present, "none"));
    }
}

A null check is used both to guard a value and to read the message. What is printed?
class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        String name = "quiz";
        String same = java.util.Objects.requireNonNull(name, "missing");
        System.out.println(name == same);
        try {
            java.util.Objects.requireNonNull((String) null, "missing");
        } catch (NullPointerException e) {
            System.out.println(e.getMessage());
        }
    }
}

The hash helper is asked about an absent reference and a number. What is printed?
class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println(java.util.Objects.hashCode(null) + " "
                + java.util.Objects.hashCode(7));
    }
}

A list holds records and a new equal record is searched for. What is printed?
record Slot(int row) {
}

class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        java.util.List<Slot> seats = java.util.List.of(new Slot(4), new Slot(7));
        System.out.println(seats.indexOf(new Slot(7)));
    }
}

A membership test is asked about a null on an immutable list. What is printed?
class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        java.util.List<String> names = java.util.List.of("a", "b");
        try {
            System.out.println(names.contains(null));
        } catch (NullPointerException e) {
            System.out.println("refused");
        }
    }
}

A builder is given a char array. What does the program print?
class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        char[] letters = { 'a', 'b' };
        StringBuilder sb = new StringBuilder("x");
        sb.append(letters);
        System.out.println(sb);
    }
}

An object with a description method is appended to a builder. What is printed?
class Coin {
    int v = 2;

    public String toString() {
        return "coin" + v;
    }
}

class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        StringBuilder sb = new StringBuilder();
        sb.append(new Coin());
        System.out.println(sb);
    }
}

Two exact decimals hold the same number written with different scales. What is printed?
class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        java.math.BigDecimal a = new java.math.BigDecimal("1.0");
        java.math.BigDecimal b = new java.math.BigDecimal("1.00");
        System.out.println(a.equals(b) + " " + a.compareTo(b));
    }
}

Two orders are compared, one by subtraction and one by the helper. What is printed?
class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int small = Integer.MIN_VALUE + 1;
        int big = Integer.MAX_VALUE;
        boolean bySubtraction = (small - big) < 0;
        boolean byHelper = Integer.compare(small, big) < 0;
        System.out.println(bySubtraction + " " + byHelper);
    }
}

An addition that leaves the range is done with the exact helper. What is printed?
class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        try {
            System.out.println(Math.addExact(Integer.MAX_VALUE, 1));
        } catch (ArithmeticException e) {
            System.out.println("overflow");
        }
    }
}

A range of an array is sorted. What does the program print?
class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int[] data = { 9, 3, 7, 1, 5 };
        java.util.Arrays.sort(data, 1, 4);
        System.out.println(java.util.Arrays.toString(data));
    }
}

An array is copied into a longer one. What does the program print?
class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int[] small = { 1, 2 };
        int[] grown = java.util.Arrays.copyOf(small, 4);
        System.out.println(java.util.Arrays.toString(grown) + " " + (small == grown));
    }
}

Two nested arrays hold equal numbers in different row objects. What is printed?
class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int[][] a = { { 1, 2 }, { 3, 4 } };
        int[][] b = { { 1, 2 }, { 3, 4 } };
        System.out.println(java.util.Arrays.equals(a, b));
    }
}

The deep comparison is used on two nested arrays. What does the program print?
class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int[][] a = { { 1, 2 }, { 3, 4 } };
        int[][] b = { { 1, 2 }, { 3, 4 } };
        b[1][0] = 9;
        System.out.println(java.util.Arrays.deepEquals(a, b) + " "
                + java.util.Arrays.deepEquals(a, a));
    }
}

A removal is attempted with a value of a different type. What does the program print?
class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        java.util.List<Object> items = new java.util.ArrayList<>();
        items.add("row");
        items.add(7);
        boolean removed = items.remove("7");
        System.out.println(removed + " " + items.size());
    }
}

A key that is absent is read with a default. What does the program print?
class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        java.util.Map<String, Integer> m = new java.util.HashMap<>();
        m.put("a", 1);
        int first = m.getOrDefault("b", -1);
        int second = m.getOrDefault("b", -1);
        System.out.println(first + " " + second + " " + m.size());
    }
}

A write is attempted on a map built by the immutable factory. What is printed?
class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        java.util.Map<String, Integer> m = java.util.Map.of("a", 1);
        try {
            m.put("b", 2);
        } catch (UnsupportedOperationException e) {
            System.out.print("refused, ");
        }
        System.out.println(m.get("a"));
    }
}

A map entry is built with a missing key or a missing value. What is printed?
class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        try {
            java.util.Map.entry(null, 1);
            System.out.println("made");
        } catch (NullPointerException e) {
            try {
                java.util.Map.entry("a", null);
                System.out.println("made twice");
            } catch (NullPointerException e2) {
                System.out.println("refused twice");
            }
        }
    }
}

A map is walked and each entry is printed. What does the program print?
class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        java.util.Map<String, Integer> m = new java.util.LinkedHashMap<>();
        m.put("a", 1);
        m.put("b", 2);
        StringBuilder sb = new StringBuilder();
        m.forEach((k, v) -> sb.append(k).append(v).append(";"));
        System.out.println(sb);
    }
}

A helper counts the elements equal to one value. What does the program print?
class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        java.util.List<String> seats = java.util.Arrays.asList("a", "b", "a", "c", "a");
        System.out.println(java.util.Collections.frequency(seats, "a"));
    }
}

A value object is changed by asking for a copy with one part replaced. What is printed?
final class Point {
    private final int x;

    Point(int x) {
        this.x = x;
    }

    int x() {
        return x;
    }

    Point withX(int newX) {
        return new Point(newX);
    }
}

class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Point a = new Point(1);
        Point b = a.withX(9);
        System.out.println(a.x() + " " + b.x() + " " + (a == b));
    }
}

A class offers a static factory beside its constructor. What does the program print?
class Money {
    private final int cents;

    private Money(int c) {
        cents = c;
    }

    static Money valueOf(int c) {
        return c < 0 ? null : new Money(c);
    }

    int cents() {
        return cents;
    }
}

class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Money good = Money.valueOf(250);
        Money bad = Money.valueOf(-1);
        System.out.println((good == null ? "none" : good.cents()) + " " + (bad == null ? "none" : "some"));
    }
}

Text that holds a bad digit is parsed as a number. What does the program print?
class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        try {
            System.out.println(Integer.parseInt("12a"));
        } catch (NumberFormatException e) {
            System.out.println("bad number");
        }
    }
}

A rejected parse is asked for its message. What does the program print?
class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        try {
            Integer.parseInt("4x");
        } catch (NumberFormatException e) {
            System.out.println(e.getMessage());
        }
    }
}

A parse in a chosen base meets a digit that base does not allow. What is printed?
class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        try {
            System.out.println(Integer.parseInt("9", 8));
        } catch (NumberFormatException e) {
            System.out.println(e.getMessage());
        }
    }
}

A character test is run on an ascii digit and on a digit from another script. What is printed?
class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        char ascii = '7';
        char other = '\u0669';
        System.out.println(Character.isDigit(ascii) + " " + Character.isDigit(other)
                + " " + (other >= '0' && other <= '9'));
    }
}

The same text is converted with two different locales. What is printed?
class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        String word = "i";
        String plain = word.toUpperCase(java.util.Locale.ROOT);
        String turkish = word.toUpperCase(java.util.Locale.forLanguageTag("tr"));
        System.out.println(plain.codePointAt(0) + " " + turkish.codePointAt(0));
    }
}

The same names are put in order two ways. What does the program print?
class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        java.util.List<String> names = java.util.Arrays.asList("Bo", "al", "Cy");
        String upperFirst = names.stream().sorted().reduce((a, b) -> a).orElse("");
        String ignoreCase = names.stream().sorted(String.CASE_INSENSITIVE_ORDER)
                .reduce((a, b) -> a).orElse("");
        System.out.println(upperFirst + " " + ignoreCase);
    }
}

Text padded with a line separator is trimmed two ways. What is printed?
class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        String padded = "\u2028quiz\u2028";
        System.out.println(padded.trim().length() + " " + padded.strip().length());
    }
}

Records are ordered by one component and ties are broken by another. What is printed?
record Slot(int row, int seat) {
}

class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        java.util.Comparator<Slot> byRowThenSeat = java.util.Comparator
                .comparingInt(Slot::row)
                .thenComparingInt(Slot::seat);
        java.util.List<Slot> seats = new java.util.ArrayList<>(
                java.util.Arrays.asList(new Slot(4, 7), new Slot(4, 2), new Slot(2, 9)));
        seats.sort(byRowThenSeat);
        StringBuilder sb = new StringBuilder();
        for (Slot s : seats) {
            sb.append(s.row()).append(":").append(s.seat()).append(" ");
        }
        System.out.println(sb.toString().trim());
    }
}
